a new initiative called power up has  been set up to support 40 black music  creators and industry professionals  the program aims to address racism in  the music industry  and achieve better representation across  all sectors  some of it is nuanced some of it is very  subtle  and then there's in your face blatant  things that happen  one of the main barriers that stuck out  to me  because i'm a black female is the um  ongoing and almost historical barrier  that black women  in music have faced  uh well one ambassador of that  initiative power up is  sabrina washington the former lead  singer of mystique she's going to be  joining us in  just a moment but first let's have a  listen back at one of their heads  i'm very pleased to say that sabrina  joins us on the program we're also  joined by power-ups programme consultant  at yahoo guys great to have you both on  the program and i'm afraid  yeah we're going to have to start with  sabrina bit of a mystique fan here in  the studio this morning  just just tell us a little bit about  about your involvement in all of this  it's  it it might seem slightly strange to  some people that  that black artist might need assistance  to to punch through or  to guard against racism given just how  influential  british black artists have been over the  past 40 50 60 years  i know it's a thing of as we can see  music is the language that everyone  speaks  but sometimes on the you know  on the lower levels and the people that  are making decisions they don't really  see  the difference or the struggle that  people of color and black go  through on a day-to-day trying to be in  the music industry  and with power up i love that it's  pushing the industry and breaking down  barriers and giving  support to up-and-coming artists that  need it  um yeah we will be with you in just a  second but sabrina tell us about then  your experience if indeed you had one  back when you were  with the girls in mystique i mean the  the strong look the great message that  you guys were putting forward  you know if we were only glancing at the  surface one might imagine that  you didn't experience any of these  problems i suspect that wasn't the case  though  no that wasn't the case we came up on a  small label an independent label that  you know saw our products and they love  what we've done  so they invested in us but major labels  they kind of just said no or said no  and we came up on a small independent  label and we came up through  the support of radio stations and djs  and  you know supporting us and put in there  you know us on the radio  and that's from them putting us on the  radio  we became mystique that everyone knows  about but  behind the scenes racism was very very  much there and i've spoken about that  on another program about racism in the  industry  and that's the only thing that we need  to  continue to break down i hope as i said  in like 10 or  20 years this is a conversation that we  should be happy because as you said  black music has always been and is still  here but it just needs support  and the artist needs support to go  forward  and feel happy about it  well let's let's bring in yahoo who was  nodding along with you there i mean what  what  what  assistance can the the power up scheme  give it's a  is it about identifying artists just  just on the cusp of success or is it  holding their hand through through the  whole process  well it's it's it's multi-faceted really  and for the participant program which we  we've just launched today  um it's about grant giving so financial  support but also  marrying that with support around the  barriers that these individuals will  face  and again it's not just for artists it's  for industry professionals  so it's looking at things like capacity  building training  network you know making sure that  there's a support network and there's a  sharing of resources  and we've got some great partners  involved in power up as well so they're  all  contributing to make sure that these  individuals get the support they deserve  and are you know to no pun intended  while powered up properly  to be able to face some of the  challenges that they are and they will  face within the music industry yeah give  us that give us an  impression or a flavor of the problems  we've heard of course from sabrina about  what it can be like for the artist  but for the executives working within  the industry as well that there are  institutional racist barriers that they  have to break through but  what are those well setting up power  we've done you know extensive research  speaking to over 80 black music  and professionals both creators and and  um  people behind the scenes and what came  out  from everybody was you know there's  there's barriers whether it just be  um you know prejudice or you know  blatant racism  but then you've got you know lack of  opportunity um  lack of you know fair resources on  projects that they may be doing whether  they are  within um a certain genre or not  and less funding goes to black leather  organizations and individuals creative  or non-creative  and it's just it's just a lot about the  unfair reflection because again  you know you said it before black  music creators and industry  professionals are contributing so much  to this industry but yet the  opportunities and the compensation  and the treatment doesn't reflect that  positive and contribution  so yet there's a myriad of um  of challenges of barriers and of  experiences which need to be addressed  i mean sabrina you you talked about you  know being centered to a smaller label  the support you got from from peers  within the community  we're going to fast forward a few years  to where we are right now it  it does almost feel like we have you  know black music creators  making their own music and being able to  get it out there through youtube through  insta  through any kind of social media i mean  they're creating their own market  in the absence of perhaps the type of  assistance that power up might be able  to provide  yeah they are i mean they've had to find  another  way in order for their music to be out  because as  you know y'all said they haven't had the  support so  what i love about power up is giving the  support  and giving a you know a  a selection of people that will help to  propel them  forward and not leave them in the same  place and  you know just break down the barriers  that need to  be broken now not tomorrow  and and i'm going to give you the last  word because we are just running out of  time  just a word from you to people who might  be watching look to the success you had  but who've also heard about the the  struggles that you and the girls had as  well i mean what reassurance would you  provide that  this is an industry you want to get in  this is an industry that you want to  succeed in  it's i say music is the language  that speaks to many many people and if  it's something that you want to do  stick to it power up is here to  help you to power you up to have  everything you need advice mentoring  from other artists you know just  mentoring in a place  of a place of peace in the industry  where you can go and you can have  conversation and you can  find out new things and get to know so  power up  is definitely a new channel that needed  to be done  and i'm glad to be a part of it so if  anyone  feels like music is their thing or  they've been trying  in music what i like about power up is  that it doesn't come with  an age of you have to only be this age  it's  wide open so if you go to prs you can go  on to power up  put in your applications and  everyone's ready to help you and that's  a good thing
 

